{#
_calendar_pane.html.twig — the calendar docked beside the mail panes.

Placement is the whole point. This is a SIBLING of .main-pane, not a child, so
the two split the row's width and the handle between them moves the boundary:
one grows exactly as much as the other shrinks. .main-pane keeps flex-1 and
absorbs whatever is left, so the split is one number — --calendar-pane-width —
and the handle only ever writes that. Two numbers would be two sources of truth
for one boundary.

Hidden below md. There, the calendar is a sidebar link to a full page that
replaces the mail view; a 380px pane next to a message list on a phone is
neither.

The width is rendered inline from the user's stored preference rather than
restored by JavaScript, so the first paint is already the right size. A pane
that appears at the default and jumps once the controller connects is worse
than one that does not remember at all.
#}

{% set paneOpen = app.user.isCalendarPaneOpen %}

{# The controller lives on the row in _mailbox.html.twig, not here: it owns the
   boundary between two panes, and its scope has to reach the sidebar's toggle
   as well as this. Visibility is one conditional class rather than
   `lg:flex lg:hidden` — those two are the same breakpoint and which wins comes
   down to CSS source order, which is not something to rely on.

   Below lg it is still this pane, not a different page — it just takes the
   whole row and the mail steps aside (see [data-calendar-mode] in app.css).
   The row cannot hold a sidebar, a usable list and a usable calendar at once
   under 1024px, so rather than squeezing both under their minimums the
   calendar takes over. That is also what the switch's third position does at
   every width. Same control, same DOM, no navigation: going back to the mail
   puts it back exactly as it was. #}
<div
    data-ui--split-target="wrapper"
    {# hidden lg:flex when remembered open: the preference is one value shared by
       every device, and below lg this pane does not sit beside the mail, it
       replaces it. Opening the calendar once at a desk should not mean every
       phone lands on it. The mail is the phone's home; the toggle still works
       and the width is still remembered. #}
    class="{{ paneOpen ? 'hidden lg:flex lg:w-auto' : 'hidden' }} shrink-0 min-w-0"
>
    {# The handle. role=separator with aria-orientation is what makes it
       reachable and meaningful without a mouse; the arrow keys are handled by
       the controller.

       Two gutters wide and pulled left by one, so it spans the whole visual
       space between the panes rather than sitting to one side of it. The row's
       own gap-gutter falls to the LEFT of this wrapper, so a handle that was
       merely one gutter wide put its grip 1.5 gutters from the mail pane and
       0.5 from the calendar — visibly off-centre, and a thin target. #}
    <div
        data-ui--split-target="handle"
        data-action="pointerdown->ui--split#start keydown->ui--split#nudge dblclick->ui--split#reset"
        role="separator"
        aria-orientation="vertical"
        aria-label="{{ 'calendar.pane.resize'|trans }}"
        tabindex="0"
        class="group relative -ml-gutter w-[calc(var(--spacing-gutter)*2)] shrink-0
               cursor-col-resize
               hidden lg:flex items-center justify-center
               focus:outline-none"
    >
        {# `.split-grip` is what the strain state in app.css grabs, so the two
           pixels of grip can say "there is more past this" while the pane is
           being dragged beyond its range. #}
        <span class="split-grip w-0.5 h-8 rounded-full bg-line
                     group-hover:bg-accent group-focus-visible:bg-accent" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </div>

    {# Width in classes rather than an inline style: inline would beat the
       breakpoint, and below lg this pane is full width rather than the stored
       one. #}
    <div
        data-ui--split-target="pane"
        class="main-pane flex flex-col overflow-hidden min-w-0
               w-full lg:w-[var(--calendar-pane-width)]"
    >
        {# A pane the user will see on first paint is rendered WITH the page —
           embedding the same controller the frame's src would have fetched —
           so a reload shows mail and calendar together instead of a spinner
           that fills in a beat later. The frame element is still the shell
           (week/view navigation inside it still swaps only the pane); it just
           starts full instead of lazy. A closed pane keeps the lazy empty
           frame: its src is set the first time the switch opens it, and
           nobody pays for a calendar they are not looking at. #}
        {% if paneOpen %}
            {{ render(controller('App\\Controller\\Calendar\\CalendarController::pane')) }}
        {% else %}
            <turbo-frame
                id="calendar-pane-frame"
                loading="lazy"
                class="flex flex-col min-h-0 flex-1"
            >
                <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-24 text-ink-faint" aria-live="polite">
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-circle-notch fa-spin" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                </div>
            </turbo-frame>
        {% endif %}
    </div>
</div>
